<?xpacket begin="ï»¿" id="W5M0MpCehiHzreSzNTczkc9d"?>
<x:xmpmeta x:xmptk="Adobe XMP Core 5.4-c006 80.159825, 2016/09/16-03:31:08" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:oasis="http://www.oasis-open.org" xmlns:pdf="http://ns.adobe.com/pdf/1.3/" xmlns:pdfx="http://ns.adobe.com/pdfx/1.3/" xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:terms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/" xmlns:x="adobe:ns:meta/" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:xmp="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/" xmlns:xmpMM="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/mm/"><rdf:RDF><rdf:Description rdf:about=""><dc:format>application/pdf</dc:format><dc:title><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Fundamental constraints to the logic of living systems</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></dc:title><dc:identifier><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">RSFS-2024-0010</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></dc:identifier><dc:creator><rdf:Seq><rdf:li>Ricard SolÃ©</rdf:li><rdf:li>Christopher P. Kempes</rdf:li><rdf:li>Bernat Corominas-Murtra</rdf:li><rdf:li>Manlio De Domenico</rdf:li><rdf:li>Artemy Kolchinsky</rdf:li><rdf:li>Michael Lachmann</rdf:li><rdf:li>Eric Libby</rdf:li><rdf:li>Serguei Saavedra</rdf:li><rdf:li>Eric Smith</rdf:li><rdf:li>David Wolpert</rdf:li></rdf:Seq></dc:creator><dc:publisher><rdf:Bag><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">The Royal Society</rdf:li></rdf:Bag></dc:publisher><dc:rightsHolder><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Â© 2024 The Authors.</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></dc:rightsHolder><dc:rights><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Published by the Royal Society under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/, which permits unrestricted use, provided the original author and source are credited.</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></dc:rights><dc:date><rdf:Seq><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">2024</rdf:li></rdf:Seq></dc:date><terms:dateCopyrighted><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">2023</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></terms:dateCopyrighted><terms:abstract><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">It has been argued that the historical nature of evolution makes it a highly path-dependent process. Under this view, the outcome of evolutionary dynamics could have resulted in organisms with different forms and functions. At the same time, there is ample evidence that convergence and constraints strongly limit the domain of the potential design principles that evolution can achieve. Are these limitations relevant in shaping the fabric of the possible? Here, we argue that fundamental constraints are associated with the logic of living matter. We illustrate this idea by considering the thermodynamic properties of living systems, the linear nature of molecular information, the cellular nature of the building blocks of life, multicellularity and development, the threshold nature of computations in cognitive systems and the discrete nature of the architecture of ecosystems. In all these examples, we present available evidence and suggest potential avenues towards a well-defined theoretical formulation.</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></terms:abstract><terms:dateSubmitted><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">20240311</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></terms:dateSubmitted><terms:dateAccepted><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">20240821</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></terms:dateAccepted><terms:isPartOf><rdf:Alt><rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">2042-8898</rdf:li></rdf:Alt></terms:isPartOf><xmpMM:DocumentID>uuid:d875aa04-7a10-42b0-92ec-259e56544348</xmpMM:DocumentID><xmpMM:InstanceID>uuid:4eb0b1e1-84c3-4daa-9b81-61536e5a1ba6</xmpMM:InstanceID></rdf:Description><rdf:Description rdf:about="">
<pdf:Producer>Antenna House PDF Output Library 7.4.1901; modified using iText 4.2.0 by 1T3XT</pdf:Producer>
